  <title>4.8.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00992</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.8.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b1ca8fa7-fb51-46df-aa63-f8fedc89858e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<para>Use the following information to answer questions 1 - 5. </para>
<blockquote>
  The temperature was recorded at several times during the day. Function \(T\) gives the temperature in degrees Fahrenheit, \(n\) hours since midnight. A graph for this function is provided.
</blockquote>
<para><figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/628c3b7693e07969ceb66d4d1b9c3ed03f8a0fd9"/></media></figure></para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="36fb9833-9b22-48d3-ba94-bfb28c0d0c2e"><problem><para>
      <para>For the interval from \(n = 1\) to \(n = 5\), determine if the average rate of change of the temperature is positive, negative, or zero.</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>negative</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is: Negative</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><para>The graph below shows the total distance, in feet, walked by a person as a function of time, in seconds. Use this information to answer questions 6 - 7.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Distance Graph"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/dbec756aaee450163ee36c17b0b92acc17a32b69"/></media></figure></para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="5cfaa9e4-f352-4656-b90d-04385efdf1c0"><problem><para>
      <para>Given the graph of total distance walked as a function of time, was the person walking faster between 20 and 40 seconds or between 80 and 100 seconds?</para>
      </para></problem><solution><para>Between 80 and 100 seconds</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is: Between 80 and 100 seconds</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
